.\" Cesar Init System — Man Page
.TH CSR 1 "2026-08-02" "csr 0.0.7" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
csr \- Cesar Init System: Sovereign PID 1 for Cudane
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csr
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B csr
is the init system for the Cudane distribution. It manages services via an
asynchronous DAG engine, handles socket activation, and provides a unified
Markdown logging system.
.br
With no command, it launches the service supervisor. Commands are grouped:
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
.TP
.B service, svc
Manage services: start (up), stop (dn), restart (rs), reload (rl), kill,
enable, disable, status, list (ls), inspect, log, cat, edit, diff, validate,
create, convert, rm, monitor, watch, and dependency tree (dep).
.TP
.B system, sys
System-level control (boot, shutdown, and related operations).
.TP
.B config, cfg
Read and write Cesar configuration.
.TP
.B log, logs
Query the unified Markdown log store.
.TP
.B socket, sock
Manage socket-activated endpoints.
.TP
.B daemon, dmon
Daemon lifecycle control.
.TP
.B snapshot, snap
Create and manage service snapshots.
.TP
.B security, sec
Security-related settings.
.TP
.B query, qry
Query service and system state.
.TP
.B debug, dbg
Diagnostic and debugging commands.
.TP
.B self, me
Self-inspection of the running supervisor.
.TP
.B plugin, plug
Manage cps plugins.
.TP
.B theme, thm
Manage cps themes.
.TP
.B tui
Manage cps TUIs.
.SH SERVICE SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B csr service start \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-w\fR] [\fI-t TIMEOUT\fR]
Start a service and its dependencies; optionally wait for readiness.
.TP
.B csr service stop \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-t TIMEOUT\fR]
Stop a service (force with \fB-f\fR).
.TP
.B csr service restart \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-t TIMEOUT\fR]
Restart a service.
.TP
.B csr service reload \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-s SIGNAL\fR]
Send a reload signal (default SIGHUP).
.TP
.B csr service kill \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-s SIGNAL\fR]
Send an arbitrary signal.
.TP
.B csr service enable \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-b\fR] [\fI-W\fR]
Enable at boot (optionally start now).
.TP
.B csr service disable \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-W\fR]
Disable at boot (optionally stop now).
.TP
.B csr service status \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-j\fR] [\fI-q\fR]
Show state (JSON or quiet).
.TP
.B csr service list [\fI-a\fR] [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-r\fR] [\fI-s KEY\fR] [\fI-j\fR] [\fI-m\fR]
List services, optionally filtered by all/failed/running.
.TP
.B csr service inspect \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-r\fR] [\fI-d\fR] [\fI-p\fR] [\fI-j\fR]
Inspect a service definition (raw config, deps, pid).
.TP
.B csr service log \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-l LINES\fR] [\fI-L LEVEL\fR] [\fI-g PATTERN\fR] [\fI-S SINCE\fR]
Follow or slice a service's Markdown log.
.TP
.B csr service create \fI-n NAME\fR \fI-e CMD\fR [\fI-r REQ\fR] [\fI-R POLICY\fR] [\fI-s SOCKET\fR] [\fI-d DESC\fR]
Create a service unit.
.TP
.B csr service convert [\fI-s SRC_DIR\fR] [\fI-d DEST_DIR\fR] [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-k\fR]
Convert systemd .service units to Cesar .ini services (default
.I /etc/systemd/system
→
.IR /etc/cesar/services ),
then remove the systemd directory (\fB-k\fR keeps it, \fB-f\fR overwrites and forces removal).
.TP
.B csr service rm \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-p\fR]
Remove a service (purge for its log too).
.TP
.B csr service monitor \fI-n NAME\fR [\fI-i MS\fR] [\fI-t THRESHOLD\fR]
Poll state every \fI-i\fR ms, alert after \fI-t\fR failures.
.TP
.B csr service watch [\fI-n NAME\fR] [\fI-e EVENTS\fR]
Watch service events.
.TP
.B csr service tree [\fI-a\fR] [\fI-f\fR] [\fI-g\fR]
Print the dependency graph (flat, or graphviz).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /system/lib/cesar/services/*.ini
System service unit definitions.
.TP
.I /etc/cesar/services/*.ini
User service unit definitions (override system units by name).
.TP
.I /etc/cesar/enabled/
Optional allowlist: when populated with .ini symlinks, only those services boot.
.TP
.I /var/log/cesar.md
Markdown log (view with \fBcsl\fR).
.TP
.I /run/cesar
Runtime state and sockets.
